00:02:03.570 --> 00:02:05.130
Now back to the show.

00:02:05.130 --> 00:02:06.120
If you're a movie buff,

00:02:06.120 --> 00:02:08.880
you've probably heard about
Leto's preferred style of acting

00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:10.170
known as the method,

00:02:10.170 --> 00:02:13.140
and it's worth examining what
method acting actually is.

00:02:13.140 --> 00:02:14.880
Today the term is basically synonymous

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:16.470
with an actor immersing themselves

00:02:16.470 --> 00:02:17.940
so deeply in their character

00:02:17.940 --> 00:02:20.040
that all distinction
functionally disappears,

00:02:20.040 --> 00:02:21.870
like Daniel Day-Lewis refusing to shower

00:02:21.870 --> 00:02:22.950
while filming "The Crucible,"

00:02:22.950 --> 00:02:24.990
or Christian Bale
basically starving himself

00:02:24.990 --> 00:02:25.890
for "The Machinist,"

00:02:25.890 --> 00:02:28.590
or Adrien Brody nuking
his entire personal life

00:02:28.590 --> 00:02:29.430
for "The Pianist."

00:02:29.430 --> 00:02:32.100
But while this style of acting
gets a lot of publicity,

00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:34.140
it has very little to
do with method acting

00:02:34.140 --> 00:02:35.610
as it was initially conceived.

00:02:35.610 --> 00:02:38.460
The term the method was created
by Russian acting teacher

00:02:38.460 --> 00:02:40.770
and scholar Konstantin Stanislavski

00:02:40.770 --> 00:02:43.200
who believed in perezhivanie,

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:44.790
or living a part.

00:02:44.790 --> 00:02:47.580
He thought actors should depict
life in all its real truth.

00:02:47.580 --> 00:02:49.560
To that end he argued that
they should incorporate

00:02:49.560 --> 00:02:51.930
their own lived experiences
into their performances

00:02:51.930 --> 00:02:53.340
via emotional memory.

00:02:53.340 --> 00:02:55.290
That is all the sensations, emotions,

00:02:55.290 --> 00:02:56.520
and physical experiences

00:02:56.520 --> 00:02:58.500
you had acquired over
the course of your life,

00:02:58.500 --> 00:03:00.540
which you could turn on
to bring greater truth

00:03:00.540 --> 00:03:01.373
to a scene.

00:03:01.373 --> 00:03:03.210
He required his students
to use the full extent

00:03:03.210 --> 00:03:05.040
of their physicality and psychology

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:06.600
and encouraged them to think deeply

00:03:06.600 --> 00:03:07.980
about their character's motivation

00:03:07.980 --> 00:03:09.120
in any given scene.

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:11.580
Every movement you make
should serve that motivation

00:03:11.580 --> 00:03:12.960
and, if channeled correctly,

00:03:12.960 --> 00:03:13.860
he believed this could create

00:03:13.860 --> 00:03:15.900
a truly transcendent performance,

00:03:15.900 --> 00:03:17.910
one in which the audience
might very well forget

00:03:17.910 --> 00:03:19.530
they're watching a performance at all.

00:03:19.530 --> 00:03:21.060
Method acting would later be adopted

00:03:21.060 --> 00:03:22.590
by American acting instructors

00:03:22.590 --> 00:03:24.450
like Lee Strasberg and Stella Adler,

00:03:24.450 --> 00:03:26.100
though they disagreed on its meaning.

00:03:26.100 --> 00:03:28.080
Strasberg emphasized psychology

00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:29.790
and tapping into effective memory

00:03:29.790 --> 00:03:31.590
via techniques like improvisation.

00:03:31.590 --> 00:03:32.790
Adler, in contrast,

00:03:32.790 --> 00:03:34.680
evolved the craft from primarily relying

00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:36.330
on personal lived experiences

00:03:36.330 --> 00:03:39.750
to prioritizing extensive
research and using imagination

00:03:39.750 --> 00:03:42.840
to immerse the actor in the
given circumstances of a role.

00:03:42.840 --> 00:03:45.360
This was in contrast to what
was going on in Hollywood

00:03:45.360 --> 00:03:46.830
where silent film stars had learned

00:03:46.830 --> 00:03:48.240
to compensate for their silence

00:03:48.240 --> 00:03:50.790
through exaggerated motions
and facial expressions.

00:03:50.790 --> 00:03:53.100
Dramatic actors behaved more like dancers,

00:03:53.100 --> 00:03:55.680
while comedians like Charlie
Chaplin or Buster Keaton

00:03:55.680 --> 00:03:57.180
pulled from traditions like Vaudeville

00:03:57.180 --> 00:04:00.090
to create visual comedy that
didn't need sound to be funny.

00:04:00.090 --> 00:04:03.000
By the time talkies rolled
around in the late 1920s,

00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:06.030
film performance had been
solidified in its abstractness

00:04:06.030 --> 00:04:08.310
and realism wasn't even desirable.

00:04:08.310 --> 00:04:10.890
What's more, Hollywood had
consolidated its dominance

00:04:10.890 --> 00:04:12.030
over national cinema

00:04:12.030 --> 00:04:14.280
and its burgeoning studio
system had established

00:04:14.280 --> 00:04:16.260
tight control over every creative choice

00:04:16.260 --> 00:04:17.340
that went into filmmaking,

00:04:17.340 --> 00:04:18.990
including acting techniques.

00:04:18.990 --> 00:04:22.290
If naturalism and silent film
was functionally impossible,

00:04:22.290 --> 00:04:23.850
once they turned the mics on

00:04:23.850 --> 00:04:26.130
it was considered
downright unprofessional.

00:04:26.130 --> 00:04:28.350
Acting natural for the
camera didn't strike anybody

00:04:28.350 --> 00:04:29.670
as particularly impressive,

00:04:29.670 --> 00:04:31.230
so actors turned it on,

00:04:31.230 --> 00:04:33.750
the most successful ones
patenting a particular character

00:04:33.750 --> 00:04:35.490
to establish their star power.

00:04:35.490 --> 00:04:37.710
Think Humphrey Bogart's world weariness

00:04:37.710 --> 00:04:40.620
or Katharine Hepburn's
airy quips and icy stares.

00:04:40.620 --> 00:04:43.710
Through typecasting, actors
were differentiated as products.

00:04:43.710 --> 00:04:45.540
For example, when Jimmy Steward repeated

00:04:45.540 --> 00:04:47.790
the same sweet-natured
optimist performance,

00:04:47.790 --> 00:04:50.730
he was reliably delivering
a product as only he could.

00:04:50.730 --> 00:04:53.220
Hollywood rumbled along like
this for a couple decades

00:04:53.220 --> 00:04:54.630
and Americans seemed cool with it.

00:04:54.630 --> 00:04:57.810
That is, until "A Streetcar
Named Desire" hit theaters.

00:04:57.810 --> 00:05:00.630
You can't overstate the
impact of Marlon Brando

00:05:00.630 --> 00:05:03.390
whose performance style
ushered in many of the greats,

00:05:03.390 --> 00:05:06.390
from James Dean to Sidney
Poitier and Meryl Streep.

00:05:06.390 --> 00:05:08.730
The reason for Brando's
sudden acclaim is simple.

00:05:08.730 --> 00:05:11.160
He was one of the first
Americans to use the method.

00:05:11.160 --> 00:05:12.660
While Cary Grant was preoccupied

00:05:12.660 --> 00:05:14.760
with acting like, well, Cary Grant,

00:05:14.760 --> 00:05:17.490
Brando was preoccupied with
capturing the unique essence

00:05:17.490 --> 00:05:18.360
of each character,

00:05:18.360 --> 00:05:20.160
both internally and eternally.

00:05:20.160 --> 00:05:22.893
- You don't understand
I could have had class.

00:05:23.838 --> 00:05:25.020
I could have been a contender.

00:05:25.944 --> 00:05:28.170
I could have been somebody.

00:05:28.170 --> 00:05:29.550
- [Hellen] Brando's acting was certainly

00:05:29.550 --> 00:05:30.630
in a league of its own,

00:05:30.630 --> 00:05:33.600
but of course in this league
there was crying in baseball.

00:05:33.600 --> 00:05:34.433
- Hey, Stella!

00:05:36.750 --> 00:05:39.300
- But he was also the right
man at the right time.

00:05:39.300 --> 00:05:40.470
Just as he was emerging,

00:05:40.470 --> 00:05:42.900
Hollywood was undergoing
a mid-century shift

00:05:42.900 --> 00:05:45.420
in the kinds of male
characters it put on screen.

00:05:45.420 --> 00:05:47.820
This meant moving away from suave actors

00:05:47.820 --> 00:05:49.170
trading on their star power

00:05:49.170 --> 00:05:51.720
and towards characters who were rugged,

00:05:51.720 --> 00:05:53.430
angry, miserable, cruel,

00:05:53.430 --> 00:05:55.470
and anti-social to the bone.

00:05:55.470 --> 00:05:58.290
It's no surprise that some
scholars attribute this trend

00:05:58.290 --> 00:06:00.840
to a need to express the
collective pain and trauma

00:06:00.840 --> 00:06:02.310
American men had experienced

00:06:02.310 --> 00:06:04.380
over the course of two world wars.

00:06:04.380 --> 00:06:06.000
Brando was the perfect avatar

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:07.770
for the very real collective suffering

00:06:07.770 --> 00:06:08.970
Americans had endured.

00:06:08.970 --> 00:06:12.000
But method acting wasn't just
about capturing the zeitgeist.

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:14.910
In addition to facilitating
more naturalistic performances,

00:06:14.910 --> 00:06:17.550
it also gave actors a
greater degree of authorship

00:06:17.550 --> 00:06:18.383
over the work.

00:06:18.383 --> 00:06:19.710
When creating his own backstories,

00:06:19.710 --> 00:06:22.020
internal monologues and
emotional experiences,

00:06:22.020 --> 00:06:24.840
an actor like Brando was
significantly adding to

00:06:24.840 --> 00:06:26.730
and sometimes even changing the script.

00:06:26.730 --> 00:06:29.280
Brando would even rewrite now iconic lines

00:06:29.280 --> 00:06:31.470
to make them feel more natural.

00:06:31.470 --> 00:06:32.700
- You come into my house

00:06:32.700 --> 00:06:34.410
on the day my daughter is to be married

00:06:34.410 --> 00:06:37.920
and you ask me to do murder, for money.

00:06:37.920 --> 00:06:40.110
- Note the way he gently cradles the cat

00:06:40.110 --> 00:06:41.550
as he plans a brutal hit,

00:06:41.550 --> 00:06:44.070
and that cat wasn't even in the script.

00:06:44.070 --> 00:06:45.270
This mother (beep).

00:06:45.270 --> 00:06:47.730
But some actors inspired
by Brando's method acting

00:06:47.730 --> 00:06:50.940
struggled with turning their
character on and off at whim.

00:06:50.940 --> 00:06:53.730
And in the 1980s, Robert
De Niro found a solution

00:06:53.730 --> 00:06:55.770
with his breakthrough
Oscar-winning performance

00:06:55.770 --> 00:06:56.640
in "Raging Bull"

00:06:56.640 --> 00:06:59.005
in which he played a
troubled former boxer.

00:06:59.005 --> 00:07:01.440
- I want you to hit me in the face.

00:07:01.440 --> 00:07:04.590
- For the role he gained 60
pounds and did in-depth research

00:07:04.590 --> 00:07:06.480
about the Italian immigrant experience.

00:07:06.480 --> 00:07:07.590
Most notably, though,

00:07:07.590 --> 00:07:10.950
he also stayed in character
for the duration of production.

00:07:10.950 --> 00:07:12.840
Did this make him miserable to work with?

00:07:12.840 --> 00:07:15.030
Well, his costar Katherine Moriarty says

00:07:15.030 --> 00:07:16.680
that it was scary to see him that way

00:07:16.680 --> 00:07:18.270
but that she had complete trust in him

00:07:18.270 --> 00:07:19.650
and never felt unsafe.

00:07:19.650 --> 00:07:22.320
So, had De Niro taken the
method to the next level?

00:07:22.320 --> 00:07:24.450
Not according to instructor Strasberg

00:07:24.450 --> 00:07:25.950
who railed against his approach.

00:07:25.950 --> 00:07:28.567
He said, "Our technique
is designed to be engaged

00:07:28.567 --> 00:07:29.467
"and then stopped.

00:07:29.467 --> 00:07:31.050
"You don't continue the reality."

00:07:31.050 --> 00:07:34.147
He added, "There is a fascination
with staying in character.

00:07:34.147 --> 00:07:35.670
"It is an anxious act"

00:07:35.670 --> 00:07:38.017
used by actors who "don't
have the confidence

00:07:38.017 --> 00:07:39.690
"to go in and out of character."

00:07:39.690 --> 00:07:41.497
He contends that "if you are committed,

00:07:41.497 --> 00:07:43.020
"you can pick it back up."

00:07:43.020 --> 00:07:47.220
- If you were to draw a graph
of my process, of my method,

00:07:47.220 --> 00:07:48.180
it would be something like this.

00:07:48.180 --> 00:07:50.970
Sir Ian, Sir Ian, Sir Ian, action.

00:07:50.970 --> 00:07:53.070
Wizard, you shall not pass!

00:07:53.070 --> 00:07:55.053
Cut, Sir Ian, Sir Ian, Sir Ian.

00:07:58.080 --> 00:08:00.000
- Say what you will about Marlon Brando,

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:01.350
but by all accounts

00:08:01.350 --> 00:08:03.180
he was able to turn off his role at night

00:08:03.180 --> 00:08:04.380
and enjoy a bowl of pasta,

00:08:04.380 --> 00:08:06.210
and De Niro's extreme interpretation

00:08:06.210 --> 00:08:08.520
or bastardization of method acting

00:08:08.520 --> 00:08:10.440
would define the term going forward.

00:08:10.440 --> 00:08:12.390
Today when you think of going method,

00:08:12.390 --> 00:08:14.220
you might think of Daniel Day-Lewis

00:08:14.220 --> 00:08:16.950
refusing to move his body
while shooting "My Left Foot,"

00:08:16.950 --> 00:08:19.110
forcing the crew to push
him around in a wheelchair

00:08:19.110 --> 00:08:20.790
in search of greater authenticity.

00:08:20.790 --> 00:08:22.860
While in production on
"Gangs of New York,"

00:08:22.860 --> 00:08:25.380
Day-Lewis wore only
period-appropriate attire

00:08:25.380 --> 00:08:27.060
on and off set,

00:08:27.060 --> 00:08:29.190
and subsequently caught pneumonia.

00:08:29.190 --> 00:08:30.780
This made for great publicity

00:08:30.780 --> 00:08:33.810
but it also contributed to the
actor's status as a legend,

00:08:33.810 --> 00:08:35.730
someone who would sacrifice anything

00:08:35.730 --> 00:08:37.860
in his quest to give an
authentic performance.

00:08:37.860 --> 00:08:40.080
It's also rumored that
he had a huge dispute

00:08:40.080 --> 00:08:41.190
with Paul Thomas Anderson

00:08:41.190 --> 00:08:42.660
while filming "There Will Be Blood"

00:08:42.660 --> 00:08:44.587
because Day-Lewis wanted to, quote,

00:08:44.587 --> 00:08:47.310
"kill Paul Dano for realsies."

00:08:47.310 --> 00:08:49.290
All of which brings us back to Leto.

00:08:49.290 --> 00:08:51.840
Now Leto has been going
method since his early days

00:08:51.840 --> 00:08:53.610
playing a heroin addict in "Requiem."

00:08:53.610 --> 00:08:56.220
For that he lost 26 pounds,
lived on the street,

00:08:56.220 --> 00:08:58.350
and stopped having sex so he would share

00:08:58.350 --> 00:09:00.450
the constant cravings of his character,

00:09:00.450 --> 00:09:02.940
which I think implies he
was as addicted to sex

00:09:02.940 --> 00:09:05.250
as a heroin user is addicted to heroin?

00:09:05.250 --> 00:09:06.900
Which is, dark?

00:09:06.900 --> 00:09:09.060
The resulting performance
was wildly acclaimed.

00:09:09.060 --> 00:09:10.140
For "Dallas Buyers Club,"

00:09:10.140 --> 00:09:11.970
he dropped 30 pounds
and stayed in character

00:09:11.970 --> 00:09:13.140
for the duration of the shoot,

00:09:13.140 --> 00:09:15.330
resulting in a performance
that landed him an Oscar.

00:09:15.330 --> 00:09:18.180
But popular opinion on
the actor started to turn

00:09:18.180 --> 00:09:20.040
and we'd be remiss if we didn't point out

00:09:20.040 --> 00:09:21.900
that at least some of the Leto backlash

00:09:21.900 --> 00:09:24.240
stems from credible
allegations of sexual assault

00:09:24.240 --> 00:09:25.200
made against the actor,

00:09:25.200 --> 00:09:28.740
which in and of itself is reason
to really not like the guy.

00:09:28.740 --> 00:09:30.780
But opinions about his acting abilities

00:09:30.780 --> 00:09:32.760
have also been on the decline lately,

00:09:32.760 --> 00:09:35.310
probably due to his
widely publicized antics

00:09:35.310 --> 00:09:36.870
on the set of "Suicide Squad."

00:09:36.870 --> 00:09:38.790
During production, Leto allegedly

00:09:38.790 --> 00:09:41.040
treated his costars abominably,

00:09:41.040 --> 00:09:44.100
sending them bizarre gifts
such as an used condom,

00:09:44.100 --> 00:09:47.130
a live rat, bullets, and anal beads.

00:09:47.130 --> 00:09:49.710
After saying so himself
in a red carpet interview,

00:09:49.710 --> 00:09:51.780
Leto has since denied these gifts.

00:09:51.780 --> 00:09:53.490
But this isn't the first time a movie star

00:09:53.490 --> 00:09:55.710
has been guilty of
disruptive on-set behavior,

00:09:55.710 --> 00:09:57.960
though sometimes such
behavior is rather delightful.

00:09:57.960 --> 00:09:59.310
George Clooney is known for pulling

00:09:59.310 --> 00:10:00.870
deliciously devious pranks

00:10:00.870 --> 00:10:02.370
and everybody on set has fun.

00:10:02.370 --> 00:10:05.070
However, what made Leto's antics so unique

00:10:05.070 --> 00:10:06.780
was his justification for them.

00:10:06.780 --> 00:10:08.610
He was trying to go full method.

00:10:08.610 --> 00:10:09.840
And guess what?

00:10:09.840 --> 00:10:11.670
It totally worked.

00:10:11.670 --> 00:10:14.703
- I am not someone who is loved.

00:10:16.830 --> 00:10:17.663
I'm an idea.

00:10:18.780 --> 00:10:20.700
- Uh, yeah, so in this case,

00:10:20.700 --> 00:10:22.560
Leto's commitment to the method

00:10:22.560 --> 00:10:24.000
doesn't seem to have helped him craft

00:10:24.000 --> 00:10:25.950
a deeper or more nuanced character.

00:10:25.950 --> 00:10:28.230
You don't get the sense
that he's carefully drawing

00:10:28.230 --> 00:10:29.430
on emotional memories,

00:10:29.430 --> 00:10:32.160
whether his own or those he's
imagined for his character.

00:10:32.160 --> 00:10:33.390
His antics mostly just seemed

00:10:33.390 --> 00:10:34.920
to have pissed off his coworkers.

00:10:34.920 --> 00:10:38.130
- He had a henchman who would
come into the rehearsal room

00:10:38.130 --> 00:10:41.310
and the henchman came in with a dead pig

00:10:41.310 --> 00:10:44.220
and plopped it on the table,

00:10:44.220 --> 00:10:45.840
and then he walked out.

00:10:45.840 --> 00:10:48.360
And that was our
introduction into Jared Leto.

00:10:48.360 --> 00:10:50.340
- In response to
criticism of his behavior,

00:10:50.340 --> 00:10:52.807
he said, "I'm an artist
at the end of the day.

00:10:52.807 --> 00:10:55.027
"If I do something risky
and you don't like it,

00:10:55.027 --> 00:10:57.330
"basically you can kiss my ass."

00:10:57.330 --> 00:10:59.880
Ah, the old "I'm an artist
so there aren't consequences

00:10:59.880 --> 00:11:01.290
to my behavior" excuse.

00:11:01.290 --> 00:11:03.900
Fantastic and very original.

00:11:03.900 --> 00:11:06.060
And we think this response reveals a lot

00:11:06.060 --> 00:11:07.620
about what method acting has become.

00:11:07.620 --> 00:11:10.140
In its early days it
was about introspection,

00:11:10.140 --> 00:11:12.180
connecting more deeply to yourself,

00:11:12.180 --> 00:11:13.950
and thus to your character.

00:11:13.950 --> 00:11:15.780
But these days it's been popularized

00:11:15.780 --> 00:11:18.060
in the form of loud outlandish stunts,

00:11:18.060 --> 00:11:20.670
none more important than doing
crazy (beep) to your body.

00:11:20.670 --> 00:11:21.540
By these standards,

00:11:21.540 --> 00:11:23.370
it seems like Steve-O and Johnny Knoxville

00:11:23.370 --> 00:11:24.990
are the method actors of our time.

00:11:24.990 --> 00:11:27.600
Indeed, in recent years
the term method has become

00:11:27.600 --> 00:11:29.910
a catchall to articulate
the courageous ends

00:11:29.910 --> 00:11:30.810
actors will go to,

00:11:30.810 --> 00:11:32.430
because these days it's not enough

00:11:32.430 --> 00:11:34.260
to just stay in character
for the whole production.

00:11:34.260 --> 00:11:36.510
See, we are a rather superficial species,

00:11:36.510 --> 00:11:39.450
so we often focus more on
actors' external transformations

00:11:39.450 --> 00:11:41.910
rather than any internal
emotional work they're doing.

00:11:41.910 --> 00:11:43.710
This has been exemplified in recent years

00:11:43.710 --> 00:11:45.150
by Oscar-baiting performances

00:11:45.150 --> 00:11:46.830
like Natalie Portman in "Black Swan"

00:11:46.830 --> 00:11:48.330
or Joaquin Phoenix in "Joker"

00:11:48.330 --> 00:11:49.740
or Heath Ledger in "The Dark Knight."

00:11:49.740 --> 00:11:51.600
What is it with dudes who play Joker?

00:11:51.600 --> 00:11:53.430
This process is what scholar Kevin Esch

00:11:53.430 --> 00:11:55.020
calls actorly transformation,

00:11:55.020 --> 00:11:56.490
which entails two things.

00:11:56.490 --> 00:11:58.590
One, transforming your body for a role,

00:11:58.590 --> 00:12:00.900
and two, doing so exclusively

00:12:00.900 --> 00:12:02.940
for "greater fidelity of performance"

00:12:02.940 --> 00:12:04.470
rather than to look a certain way.

00:12:04.470 --> 00:12:06.300
Indeed, these bodily transformations

00:12:06.300 --> 00:12:08.490
are often in service of
constructing a character

00:12:08.490 --> 00:12:10.710
that is ugly or revolting.

00:12:10.710 --> 00:12:13.020
These performances
garner the most acclaim,

00:12:13.020 --> 00:12:15.420
presumably because we
see these actors as brave

00:12:15.420 --> 00:12:17.730
for sacrificing their
most priced possession,

00:12:17.730 --> 00:12:19.470
their super-human hotness.

00:12:19.470 --> 00:12:21.240
Esch argues that this perception leads us

00:12:21.240 --> 00:12:23.190
to overvalue such performances

00:12:23.190 --> 00:12:25.170
simply for their physical fidelity.

00:12:25.170 --> 00:12:26.250
Did we mention that people can be

00:12:26.250 --> 00:12:28.110
a little superficial sometimes?

00:12:28.110 --> 00:12:29.670
But it's not just about looks.

00:12:29.670 --> 00:12:32.490
Modern method is also rife
with outrageous antics,

00:12:32.490 --> 00:12:35.640
exemplified by folks
like Leto or, say, Leo,

00:12:35.640 --> 00:12:37.860
who at least to his credit
typically goes method

00:12:37.860 --> 00:12:39.780
for movies that are actually good.

00:12:39.780 --> 00:12:41.640
Take DiCaprio slicing his hand open

00:12:41.640 --> 00:12:43.170
during "Django Unchained."

00:12:43.170 --> 00:12:45.450
That's right, he bled real blood,

00:12:45.450 --> 00:12:48.300
supposedly cementing his
complete immersion in the role.

00:12:48.300 --> 00:12:50.970
But Leo hit the peak of
actual self-flagellation

00:12:50.970 --> 00:12:52.170
in "The Revenant."

00:12:52.170 --> 00:12:54.150
This man deliberately chose

00:12:54.150 --> 00:12:56.370
to submerge himself in freezing waters

00:12:56.370 --> 00:12:57.810
and eat raw bison liver

00:12:57.810 --> 00:12:59.400
in order to win his first Oscar,

00:12:59.400 --> 00:13:01.770
and his win sent a simple message.

00:13:01.770 --> 00:13:04.440
A real actor destroys
themselves for their art.

00:13:04.440 --> 00:13:06.720
Which is why I drink a
tiny teaspoon of poison

00:13:06.720 --> 00:13:08.760
before shooting every Wisecrack video.

00:13:08.760 --> 00:13:10.350
Arguably, "The Revenant" is far

00:13:10.350 --> 00:13:12.180
from DiCaprio's best performance,

00:13:12.180 --> 00:13:14.580
but it certainly was the
most publicity-generating.

00:13:14.580 --> 00:13:16.770
This furthers our
suspicion that publicity,

00:13:16.770 --> 00:13:18.930
rather than elevation
of acting as a craft,

00:13:18.930 --> 00:13:20.970
has become the point of
this distorted version

00:13:20.970 --> 00:13:21.803
of method acting,

00:13:21.803 --> 00:13:24.030
and hardcore method actors don't only risk

00:13:24.030 --> 00:13:25.380
harming their only bodies.

00:13:25.380 --> 00:13:28.110
Even Brando infamously
took method acting too far

00:13:28.110 --> 00:13:30.150
while filming "Last Tango in Paris."

00:13:30.150 --> 00:13:31.080
Though it was made before

00:13:31.080 --> 00:13:33.390
the job intimacy coordinator existed,

00:13:33.390 --> 00:13:36.360
surprising your costar with
unplanned sexual contact

00:13:36.360 --> 00:13:37.860
is tantamount to assault,

00:13:37.860 --> 00:13:40.650
and Brando's not the only
actor who's harmed a costar

00:13:40.650 --> 00:13:41.880
in the name of method acting.

00:13:41.880 --> 00:13:43.920
Take Dustin Hoffman slapping Meryl Streep

00:13:43.920 --> 00:13:45.180
in "Kramer vs. Kramer"

00:13:45.180 --> 00:13:47.730
or Jamie Dornan preparing
to play a serial killer

00:13:47.730 --> 00:13:49.560
by actually stalking random women

00:13:49.560 --> 00:13:51.990
who definitely were not
thanked in the credits.

00:13:51.990 --> 00:13:53.010
Sheesh, dude.

00:13:53.010 --> 00:13:55.590
As a result of the emphasis
on physical transformation

00:13:55.590 --> 00:13:56.940
and outrageous stunts,

00:13:56.940 --> 00:13:59.130
critics and audiences
have become more prime

00:13:59.130 --> 00:14:01.140
to appreciate the torment and extremes

00:14:01.140 --> 00:14:02.130
the actor went through

00:14:02.130 --> 00:14:04.530
as opposed to the quality
of the performance itself.

00:14:04.530 --> 00:14:06.000
This type of appreciation demands

00:14:06.000 --> 00:14:07.650
a dual perspective from viewers,

00:14:07.650 --> 00:14:10.140
one that requires them to
be simultaneously invested

00:14:10.140 --> 00:14:11.430
in the fantasy of the film

00:14:11.430 --> 00:14:12.900
while also being hyperaware

00:14:12.900 --> 00:14:14.670
that they're watching an actor at work.

00:14:14.670 --> 00:14:16.470
And the most outrageous thing about this,

00:14:16.470 --> 00:14:18.270
beside the hassles or bison liver,

00:14:18.270 --> 00:14:19.710
is that it goes against everything

00:14:19.710 --> 00:14:22.290
that method acting was meant
to be in the first place.

00:14:22.290 --> 00:14:24.060
Method was about transcendence,

00:14:24.060 --> 00:14:25.560
about losing sight of the difference

00:14:25.560 --> 00:14:27.150
between the actor and the character.

00:14:27.150 --> 00:14:29.190
When it becomes fuel for media frenzy,

00:14:29.190 --> 00:14:32.190
all we do is hyper-focus on
the actor doing the role.

00:14:32.190 --> 00:14:34.830
Leo didn't disappear into his
character in "The Revenant."

00:14:34.830 --> 00:14:36.780
If anything, the character disappeared

00:14:36.780 --> 00:14:38.550
into his awesome star power.

00:14:38.550 --> 00:14:40.200
The only role playing going on

00:14:40.200 --> 00:14:43.530
was that of Leo as a strong
man who suffers for art.

00:14:43.530 --> 00:14:45.690
So, what makes Leo's work revelatory

00:14:45.690 --> 00:14:47.100
and Leto's work a punchline?

00:14:47.100 --> 00:14:48.720
Well, perhaps because Leto's forcing

00:14:48.720 --> 00:14:50.520
that type of intensity upon others

00:14:50.520 --> 00:14:52.500
and doing so for low-stakes blockbusters

00:14:52.500 --> 00:14:53.850
rather than prestige films.

00:14:53.850 --> 00:14:56.160
While Daniel Day-Lewis
probably smelled awful

00:14:56.160 --> 00:14:57.210
on the set of "The Crucible,"

00:14:57.210 --> 00:14:59.790
he still had to bear the
brunt of his acting choices.

00:14:59.790 --> 00:15:01.200
And when Leo cut his hand,

00:15:01.200 --> 00:15:03.600
he dealt with the scar, not his coworkers.

00:15:03.600 --> 00:15:05.190
However, in an era where we're learning

00:15:05.190 --> 00:15:07.980
to interrogate the filmmaking
practices of those in power,

00:15:07.980 --> 00:15:10.200
Jared Leto variously inconveniencing

00:15:10.200 --> 00:15:12.930
or straight up terrorizing
others in the name of acting

00:15:12.930 --> 00:15:14.160
seems pretty unappealing.

00:15:14.160 --> 00:15:15.870
- Margot opened the box

00:15:15.870 --> 00:15:18.780
and there was a live rat in the box.

00:15:18.780 --> 00:15:20.520
- For example, on the set of "Morbius,"

00:15:20.520 --> 00:15:22.770
they had to ask Leto
to stop using crutches

00:15:22.770 --> 00:15:23.760
to use the bathroom

00:15:23.760 --> 00:15:25.590
and use a wheelchair instead,

00:15:25.590 --> 00:15:28.320
as his commitment to method urination

00:15:28.320 --> 00:15:29.910
was putting them behind schedule.

00:15:29.910 --> 00:15:31.410
Despite its glamorization,

00:15:31.410 --> 00:15:34.050
acting is still at the
end of the day a job,

00:15:34.050 --> 00:15:35.070
and like any job,

00:15:35.070 --> 00:15:37.290
there should be a baseline
of acceptable behavior

00:15:37.290 --> 00:15:38.730
and acceptable sacrifice.

00:15:38.730 --> 00:15:40.800
If being a dick to her
administrative assistant

00:15:40.800 --> 00:15:42.840
was part of your accountant's process,

00:15:42.840 --> 00:15:44.580
you'd probably switch to TurboTax.

00:15:44.580 --> 00:15:47.580
But when an actor behaves a
dick to get into character,

00:15:47.580 --> 00:15:48.690
it's often seen as proof

00:15:48.690 --> 00:15:50.250
of their dedication to their craft.

00:15:50.250 --> 00:15:52.320
What's more, we don't
expect most professionals

00:15:52.320 --> 00:15:55.170
to endure ongoing physical
pain or emotional torment

00:15:55.170 --> 00:15:56.520
just to be good at their jobs.

00:15:56.520 --> 00:15:59.250
But because this suffering
artist trope is so prominent,

00:15:59.250 --> 00:16:01.890
we valorize great art
being created out of pain

00:16:01.890 --> 00:16:04.290
as a type of spiritual mystical endeavor,

00:16:04.290 --> 00:16:07.410
and method has become the
easiest way to measure that pain.

00:16:07.410 --> 00:16:09.060
So, is method acting dead

00:16:09.060 --> 00:16:11.220
and did Jared Leto kill
it by being an asshole,

00:16:11.220 --> 00:16:14.340
or has method just become a
blanket excuse to be an asshole?

00:16:14.340 --> 00:16:17.160
Fellow method dabbler Robert
Pattinson suspects as such,

00:16:17.160 --> 00:16:19.987
explaining that "you only
ever see people do the method

00:16:19.987 --> 00:16:21.337
"when they're playing an asshole.

00:16:21.337 --> 00:16:23.377
"You never see someone
being lovely to everyone

00:16:23.377 --> 00:16:25.080
"while they're really deep in character."

00:16:25.080 --> 00:16:26.970
And while that may be functionally true,

00:16:26.970 --> 00:16:29.340
we'd argue that the method
as originally conceived

00:16:29.340 --> 00:16:30.360
is alive and well,

00:16:30.360 --> 00:16:32.670
if perhaps not given the
bulk of critical attention.

00:16:32.670 --> 00:16:35.310
For example, Rosamund Pike
who famously portrayed

00:16:35.310 --> 00:16:36.420
Amy in "Gone Girl"

00:16:36.420 --> 00:16:39.180
subscribes to method acting
but not the full immersion

00:16:39.180 --> 00:16:41.070
of some of its more extreme adherence.

00:16:41.070 --> 00:16:42.937
She says, "During the making of a film,

00:16:42.937 --> 00:16:45.217
"there are two people living side by side.

00:16:45.217 --> 00:16:47.077
"There is you and there is the character.

00:16:47.077 --> 00:16:48.727
"As you become more and more familiar

00:16:48.727 --> 00:16:50.497
"in that character's head space and shoes,

00:16:50.497 --> 00:16:52.927
"you become more deft at being
able to switch in and out,

00:16:52.927 --> 00:16:55.560
"but at the beginning you
always need a way in."

00:16:55.560 --> 00:16:58.500
To get into Amy's head, she
made a special music playlist.

00:16:58.500 --> 00:17:01.350
For another role, she would
shadowbox in between takes.

00:17:01.350 --> 00:17:02.310
While playing Amy,

00:17:02.310 --> 00:17:04.800
she tried to channel
the character's narcism,

00:17:04.800 --> 00:17:06.510
constantly asking herself,

00:17:06.510 --> 00:17:08.790
what effect am I having on this person?

00:17:08.790 --> 00:17:11.220
Are they liking me, are
they impressed by me?

00:17:11.220 --> 00:17:12.510
Watching her in "Gone Girl,"

00:17:12.510 --> 00:17:14.790
you can sense her doing what Stanislavski

00:17:14.790 --> 00:17:16.350
instructed actors to do,

00:17:16.350 --> 00:17:18.780
turning remembered feelings into poetry.

00:17:18.780 --> 00:17:20.790
Interestingly enough, the ongoing conflict

00:17:20.790 --> 00:17:23.670
between youthful dedication to
method's more extreme antics

00:17:23.670 --> 00:17:26.460
compared to the quiet
focus of experienced acting

00:17:26.460 --> 00:17:28.350
is best embodied today in the discourse

00:17:28.350 --> 00:17:31.470
about "Succession" actors
Brian Cox and Jeremy Strong.

00:17:31.470 --> 00:17:33.210
Strong's approach to acting was profiled

00:17:33.210 --> 00:17:34.620
in a viral New Yorker piece,

00:17:34.620 --> 00:17:37.830
and though he identifies as
immersive rather than method,

00:17:37.830 --> 00:17:39.840
he seems to employ a lot
of the same techniques.

00:17:39.840 --> 00:17:42.450
For example, he distances
himself from his costars

00:17:42.450 --> 00:17:44.640
to mimic his character's
strained relations

00:17:44.640 --> 00:17:47.370
and requested to get actually
tear-gassed in a scene

00:17:47.370 --> 00:17:48.720
just for the authenticity.

00:17:48.720 --> 00:17:51.060
Cox, a classically-trained Scottish actor,

00:17:51.060 --> 00:17:52.590
finds this approach concerning.

00:17:52.590 --> 00:17:55.110
Calling method acting
an "American disease,"

00:17:55.110 --> 00:17:57.240
he personally ascribes to a turn it on,

00:17:57.240 --> 00:17:58.770
turn it off style of acting

00:17:58.770 --> 00:18:00.630
that frankly sounds a lot less miserable.

00:18:00.630 --> 00:18:03.000
So, is it time to abandon method acting?

00:18:03.000 --> 00:18:04.920
Maybe for the Jared Letos of the world.

00:18:04.920 --> 00:18:07.110
On a small scale, Leto was no different

00:18:07.110 --> 00:18:09.270
than anyone who thinks their
job is important enough

00:18:09.270 --> 00:18:11.730
to justify making their
coworkers' lives miserable.

00:18:11.730 --> 00:18:14.190
His problem is less that
he's taking method too far

00:18:14.190 --> 00:18:16.080
and more that he's just being a jerk.

00:18:16.080 --> 00:18:18.120
And all the attention he gets for doing so

00:18:18.120 --> 00:18:20.010
undermines the actors who can use method

00:18:20.010 --> 00:18:23.220
to carefully craft
psychologically rich performances.

00:18:23.220 --> 00:18:25.530
Overall, it seems to us
that a technique invented

00:18:25.530 --> 00:18:27.750
to facilitate more authentic performances

00:18:27.750 --> 00:18:29.640
is now overwhelmingly drawing attention

00:18:29.640 --> 00:18:32.670
to the actor as a performer
rather than as a character.

00:18:32.670 --> 00:18:34.620
Of course, method acting can still be used

00:18:34.620 --> 00:18:37.080
to add quiet truths to
a skilled actor's work.

00:18:37.080 --> 00:18:39.993
- Our spirits live on
in the whole of time,

00:18:40.980 --> 00:18:44.280
exist in many vessels through time.

00:18:44.280 --> 00:18:45.150
- That just doesn't make

00:18:45.150 --> 00:18:47.370
for the spiciest "Entertainment
Weekly" headline.

00:18:47.370 --> 00:18:49.920
But we'd like to give the last
word to Sir Laurence Olivier

00:18:49.920 --> 00:18:51.690
who, after learning that his costar

00:18:51.690 --> 00:18:53.670
in "Marathon Man" Dustin Hoffman

00:18:53.670 --> 00:18:55.260
had gone three days without sleeping

00:18:55.260 --> 00:18:56.820
to get into character,

00:18:56.820 --> 00:18:58.987
allegedly asked, "My dear boy,

00:18:58.987 --> 00:19:00.990
"why don't you just try acting?"

00:19:00.990 --> 00:19:02.550
But what do you guys think?

00:19:02.550 --> 00:19:04.350
Does modern method acting need to smoke

00:19:04.350 --> 00:19:05.700
and or touch some grass?

00:19:05.700 --> 00:19:08.580
Is Jared Leto spoiling method
acting for everyone else?

00:19:08.580 --> 00:19:10.770
Or is fully submerging
yourself in your character

00:19:10.770 --> 00:19:12.120
still the best way to achieve

00:19:12.120 --> 00:19:13.770
an authentic onscreen performance?

00:19:13.770 --> 00:19:15.180
Let us know in the comments.

00:19:15.180 --> 00:19:16.980
Big thanks to our Patrons
for all your support

00:19:16.980 --> 00:19:18.570
and be sure to check out the new perks

00:19:18.570 --> 00:19:20.610
on our jazzed up Patreon page.

00:19:20.610 --> 00:19:21.900
Gnaw on that subscribe button

00:19:21.900 --> 00:19:23.640
like it's some delicious bison liver

00:19:23.640 --> 00:19:25.590
and don't forget to ring that bell.

00:19:25.590 --> 00:19:29.375
And as always, thanks for watching, later.

00:19:29.375 --> 00:19:31.958
(bright music)

